# Runtime state, not source. The root .gitignore already draws this line for the
# journal ("your own trade history, not source"); these are the paper trader's
# equivalents and belong on the same side of it.

# The live paper portfolio: cash, open positions, equity curve. This is your
# trading state. Committing it means every session's positions land in the
# history, and a checkout on another machine would silently restore someone
# else's book.
#
# Globbed rather than named exactly, because the exact name was not the only
# one that ever holds this data. `Book.save` writes `paper_book.json.tmp` and
# moves it into place, so an interrupted save leaves one behind; and repairing
# a damaged book means staging the rebuilt copy beside the original before
# swapping it in. Both are the same portfolio under a different suffix, and the
# exact-match rule let them show up as ordinary untracked files — one `git add`
# away from putting the whole book in the history the rule above exists to keep
# it out of.
data/paper_book.json*

# Books retired by `run.py paper --reset`. Same reasoning as the live book, and
# they are the evidence that a track record was not quietly restarted — which
# only means anything while they stay on the machine that produced them.
data/paper_book-archived-*.json

# Caches, rebuilt on demand. The liquidity cache is ~1 MB of scratch that exists
# only so a rate-limited screen can resume; the universe is re-screened weekly.
data/paper_universe.json
data/paper_liquidity_partial.json

# What THIS account's IBKR subscriptions can price. Measured against the logged-in
# account, so it is state rather than source: a different account has different
# entitlements, and committing one would tell the next checkout that instruments
# it cannot price are tradable.
data/ibkr_coverage.json

# The expensive artefact of a full study — tens of MB of raw candidates, kept so
# re-analysis does not need another replay. Reproducible from tools/full_study.py.
# Globbed, because each study mode writes its own file (FULL-STUDY-MACRO-...).
reports/FULL-STUDY*-candidates.json

# Universe history reused between same-day rebalances. Tens of MB of price
# frames, rebuilt on demand from IBKR — a cache, not source.
data/universe_history_cache.pkl

# The most recent scan, so any process can render it. Rebuilt by every scan.
data/last_scan.json

# Closed trades, moved out of the book so it describes only what is held.
# Append-only history; the book keeps a short tail that is a copy of these rows.
# Globbed for the same reason as the book above: a rebuilt copy staged next to
# the original is still your trade history.
data/closed_trades.jsonl*

# Generated public site. Rebuilt by `run.py publish` and after every scheduled run.
public/

# Raw intraday sweep output. The FINDING lives in reports/INTRADAY-REVIEW.md,
# which is a few kilobytes and is what anyone would actually read; these are
# 1.3 MB each and 880 KB of that is a per-instrument block for 1,554 names.
# Reproducible in about two minutes of CPU from the cached bars, against the
# seven hours of broker time the original run cost — which is precisely why
# the bar cache exists.
reports/INTRADAY-*.json

# Today's intraday working set: which sixty instruments the live loop polls.
# Rebuilt every morning from measured spreads, and account-specific — a
# different subscription selects a different set. State, not source.
data/intraday_watchlist.json
